# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What never enters the Docker build context.
#
# Both Dockerfiles do `COPY . .` into their `source` stage — see the header of
# Dockerfile.api for why a hand-written per-package COPY list was rejected — so
# this file, and only this file, decides what the build can see. Everything below
# is either useless in an image (host build output), harmful in an image (secrets,
# deployment material) or embarrassing in an image (repository prose).
#
# ## Patterns are matched against the WHOLE relative path
#
# BuildKit matches each pattern against the path relative to the context root, so
# a pattern with no separator in it matches only files at that root. `*.md` is one
# path segment and matched `README.md` alone; `apps/web/AGENTS.md` and
# `apps/web/CLAUDE.md` sailed straight past it into the context, past
# Dockerfile.web's prune (a deny list of source directories, which does not name
# markdown), and into the published web image through
# `COPY --from=build /app/apps/web /app/apps/web`. The repository's agent
# instructions shipped to anyone who pulled it.
#
# Every rule here that must hold at depth therefore carries a `**/` twin. The
# unqualified spelling is kept beside it rather than replaced: `**/x` is the wider
# claim, and keeping both makes it obvious that the root case was considered.
#
# Verify a change to this file the way the defect above was found, which needs no
# application build:
#
#     printf 'FROM busybox:latest\nWORKDIR /ctx\nCOPY . .\n' > /tmp/ctx.Dockerfile
#     docker build -q -f /tmp/ctx.Dockerfile -t prl-ctxprobe .
#     docker run --rm --entrypoint sh prl-ctxprobe -c 'find /ctx -name "*.md" -o -name ".env*"'
#
# `pnpm run release:artifacts` makes the same check against the BUILT image on
# every release build, which is the check that matters: this file could be right
# and a Dockerfile could still copy something in.
#
# ## What is NOT excluded, on purpose
#
# `scripts/`, `.github/`, `db/`, tsconfigs and the test trees all still enter the
# context and are removed inside the images by the prune stages. That duplication
# is deliberate: the prunes are deny lists whose worst failure is a fat image,
# while an exclusion here is invisible at build time and its worst failure is a
# `COPY` that silently copies nothing. Only the things that must never be in a
# layer at all are listed here.
#
# That paragraph was, for a while, not true. This file said the tsconfigs and the
# test trees were removed by the prune stages; the released images said otherwise.
# prl-web shipped `/app/apps/web/test` — 26 files, 320 KB — because the runtime
# stage copies `apps/web` from the build stage OVER the pruned prod-deps tree and
# the build stage's prune had no `apps/web/test` line. Both images shipped every
# `tsconfig.test.json`, because the prunes globbed `tsconfig.json`, which does not
# match it. prl-api additionally shipped `apps/web/next.config.ts` and
# `next-env.d.ts`, because the prune named app SUBdirectories and nothing at the
# app root. All three are fixed in the Dockerfiles, which is where the fix belongs
# — the reason none of it is excluded here has not changed. It is worth reading as
# a demonstration of this paragraph's own risk: a claim about a prune is only as
# true as the prune, and nothing failed while it was false.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Host build output and installed dependencies. Every stage installs from the
# lockfile; a macOS node_modules copied into a Linux image is worse than useless.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.next
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Version-control history, which carries every secret anyone ever committed and
# then removed.
.git

# Secrets. `.env` and `.env.*` are one path segment each and so covered only the
# repository root; a developer's `apps/api/.env` would have entered the context
# and, through the wholesale `COPY /app/apps/web`, shipped. Same defect as the
# `*.md` entry below, one directory deeper.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Deployment inputs. `scripts/staging.mjs` writes a local CA, a leaf certificate,
# its private key and an environment file holding the session secret under
# deploy/staging/. None of that is build input and all of it is credential
# material; deploy/staging/.gitignore keeps it out of git, and this keeps it out
# of every image layer.
deploy

# Prose, including the agent instruction files this rule exists for. `docs` is a
# root-level directory; the two markdown patterns are the root and the depth case.
docs
*.md
**/*.md
